## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 5 Critical · 0 High · 1 Medium · 1 Low**

This suite has a systemic self-referential assertion problem. Five of eight tests assert `output == input` for operations that should transform data, making them tautological — they pass regardless of whether the production code works correctly.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests giving false confidence

#### 1. Self-referential assertion — `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 73‑77)

Input is already `"HELLO"`. `ToUpper()` is a no-op, so `Assert.AreEqual(input, result)` is trivially true.

```csharp
// BEFORE — always passes, tests nothing
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — actually verifies transformation
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

#### 2. Self-referential assertion — `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 39‑41)

`Normalize()` does `Trim().ToLowerInvariant()`. Input `"alice"` is already trimmed and lowercase, so the assertion is trivially true.

```csharp
// BEFORE — input survives Normalize untouched
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER — verify the transformation actually happens
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

If you also want a "no-change" case, keep it but **add** a transforming case — the no-change test alone proves nothing.

---

#### 3. Self-referential assertion — `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 59‑60)

`GetDisplayName()` returns `Name` verbatim. The test asserts `user.Name == display`, which is `user.Name == user.Name` — tautological.

```csharp
// BEFORE — asserts a field against itself
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER — assert against a literal expected value
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
```

If `GetDisplayName` is ever changed to format (e.g., `"Alice (30)"`), the literal-based test will catch it; the self-referential version never will.

---

#### 4. Self-referential assertion — `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 66‑68)

`ValidateEmail` returns the input on success. The test asserts `input == result`, which is inherently true for any valid input. More critically, there is **no test for the failure path** — the exception on invalid email is completely unverified.

```csharp
// BEFORE — only checks identity on happy path
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER — verify the real contract: valid returns input, invalid throws
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_ValidInput_ReturnsInput()
{
    var result = Validator.ValidateEmail("hello@world.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("hello@world.com", result);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAt_ThrowsArgumentException()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(
        () => Validator.ValidateEmail("no-at-sign"));
}
```

---

#### 5. Self-referential round-trip — `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 47‑52)

The mapping is **lossy by design**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`, hardcodes `Age = 0`. `ToEntity` drops `Age`, hardcodes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. The test asserts `entity.Id == backToEntity.Id` (1 vs 0) and `entity.Email == backToEntity.Email` (`"a@b.com"` vs `""`), which **should fail**. If it passes, something else is wrong; if it fails, it's a broken test. Either way, a round-trip assertion on a lossy mapping is the wrong approach.

```csharp
// AFTER — test each direction independently against known values
[TestMethod]
public void ToDto_MapsFullNameToName()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ToEntity_MapsNameToFullName()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email);
}
```

---

### 🟡 Medium

| # | Anti-Pattern | Location | Issue |
|---|---|---|---|
| 6 | **Missing failure-path coverage** | Entire file | Not a single test verifies an error/exception path. `Validator.ValidateEmail` throws on bad input — untested. `Money.Parse` will throw on malformed strings — untested. `FromJson<T>` on garbage JSON — untested. |

---

### 🟢 Low

| # | Anti-Pattern | Location | Issue |
|---|---|---|---|
| 7 | **Poor naming** | `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` | `_Works` is meaningless. Prefer `ToUpper_LowercaseInput_ReturnsUppercase`. |

---

### ✅ Positive observations

